1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,020

2
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:08,230
Today, Irwan and I are going
to be giving a talk on scaling

3
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:09,547
transformers through sparsity.

4
00:00:09,547 --> 00:00:12,130
And the kind of sparsity we're
going to be talking about today

5
00:00:12,130 --> 00:00:16,530
is the kind where each input
can get either a different set

6
00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:19,030
of weights or have a different
amount of computation applied

7
00:00:19,030 --> 00:00:20,200
to it.

8
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,330
Irwan, do you want
to start it off?

9
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:24,460
Yep.

10
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:27,160
So I guess, the
overall motivation

11
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:31,590
for this line of work is
that the community has

12
00:00:31,590 --> 00:00:34,510
kind of realized
that scale is perhaps

13
00:00:34,510 --> 00:00:38,680
one of the most important
axes to focus on for obtaining

14
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:39,970
strong performance.

15
00:00:39,970 --> 00:00:43,717
And there's almost this
sort of ongoing arms race

16
00:00:43,717 --> 00:00:46,050
right now with different labs
and different institutions

17
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:53,290
sort of competing for
training the largest models.

18
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:58,450
And so maybe this dates back
from early 2020 with a paper

19
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:03,700
from OpenAI called "Scaling Laws
for Neural Language Models,"

20
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,870
where they find that
model performance follows

21
00:01:07,870 --> 00:01:12,117
a predictable power law,
scales sort of as a power

22
00:01:12,117 --> 00:01:17,450
law with model size in terms
of either compute, also

23
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:19,490
just parameters.

24
00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:22,020

25
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:25,400
And so this scaling
law kind of generalizes

26
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:27,690
over multiple
orders of magnitude.

27
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:30,110
And that gives us the
confidence that if we

28
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:33,290
are to train very
large models, we

29
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:39,200
can expect performance just
by extrapolating these scaling

30
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,070
laws.

31
00:01:41,070 --> 00:01:47,420
So in that paper, they also
find the interesting observation

32
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:52,070
that, basically, larger models
are more sample-efficient.

33
00:01:52,070 --> 00:01:58,840
And so if you have a
fixed compute budget,

34
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:03,010
you can predict what
is the size, what

35
00:02:03,010 --> 00:02:06,040
is the optimal model size,
for a fixed compute budget.

36
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:11,860
And the overall
observation is that you'd

37
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:15,670
rather train very large
models for less tests

38
00:02:15,670 --> 00:02:19,600
than train smaller models
on more training steps.

39
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:24,730
And so these models
are scaled through--

40
00:02:24,730 --> 00:02:27,535
basically, the paper
focuses on dense models,

41
00:02:27,535 --> 00:02:31,720
right, where you just
increase the model dimensions.

42
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:34,430
But they're not
looking at sparsity.

43
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:37,120
And so sparsity
is a new dimension

44
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:40,120
that you can use to
scale architectures.

45
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:45,130
And this is sort of
the focus of the talk.

46
00:02:45,130 --> 00:02:48,820
And so the sparsity we are
mentioning here is basically,

47
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:54,220
you will have sparsely
activated weights

48
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:56,800
based on the network inputs.

49
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:00,280
So every input will go to
a roughly similar amount

50
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:05,770
of computation but will be
applied different weights.

51
00:03:05,770 --> 00:03:10,540
And so this dates back
to 1991 with a paper

52
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:13,900
called "Adaptive Mixtures
of Local Experts"

53
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:17,530
and was recently revisited by
Noam Shazeer and colleagues

54
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:23,110
at Google Brain with
LSTMs, where they replaced

55
00:03:23,110 --> 00:03:28,540
some of the feedforward
networks and LSTMs

56
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:30,670
with mixture of experts.

57
00:03:30,670 --> 00:03:32,500
And so the way this
works, or roughly,

58
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:37,360
is that you will have multiple
experts, each implementing

59
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:40,360
a small network or,
in that case, I think,

60
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:45,890
just a dense matrix
multiplication.

61
00:03:45,890 --> 00:03:50,440
And so you have an additional
gating network, shown in green

62
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:57,610
here, that outputs a probability
distribution of our experts

63
00:03:57,610 --> 00:04:02,150
that each token
should be sent to.

64
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:06,610
So this probability distribution
is computed as a softmax.

65
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:11,620
And once you have it,
you select a few experts.

66
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:13,960
So there are
different strategies.

67
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:15,880
Maybe we'll talk
about it later on.

68
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:18,700
And the output is simply
sort of the weighted mixture

69
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:20,725
of all selected
experts' outputs.

70
00:04:20,725 --> 00:04:25,210

71
00:04:25,210 --> 00:04:29,142
So they've been
pretty successful,

72
00:04:29,142 --> 00:04:33,220
primarily in translation.

73
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:41,760
But there were some complexities
that have a broader use in NLP.

74
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:46,440
And so the switch transformer
paper addresses some of those.

75
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:52,890
And we'll be discussing how
to fix training instabilities

76
00:04:52,890 --> 00:04:59,200
or reduce communication costs
and reduce model complexity.

77
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:00,835
All right, Barret,
do you want to go?

78
00:05:00,835 --> 00:05:02,440
Sure, yeah.

79
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:03,910
So one kind of
approach that we're

80
00:05:03,910 --> 00:05:07,480
going to have for sparsity
is the switch transformer,

81
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:10,870
which is kind of like a
simplified mixture of expert

82
00:05:10,870 --> 00:05:14,080
variants, along with some other
improved training and fine

83
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:17,770
tuning techniques that allow
it to be stably trained

84
00:05:17,770 --> 00:05:20,260
and also perform
better when fine tuned

85
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:22,420
on a lot of downstream tasks.

86
00:05:22,420 --> 00:05:26,528
And so the switch
transformer kind of model

87
00:05:26,528 --> 00:05:27,570
works with the following.

88
00:05:27,570 --> 00:05:29,320
So you have some
transformer model

89
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:32,800
that has self-attention
and feedforward layers.

90
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:35,350
And the idea is that we replace
maybe one every two or one

91
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:38,890
every four feedforward layers
with a switch transformer

92
00:05:38,890 --> 00:05:40,630
layer.

93
00:05:40,630 --> 00:05:44,890
So you can see on the left is
one kind of layer block, which

94
00:05:44,890 --> 00:05:47,170
is self-attention,
and add normalize,

95
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:49,390
then a feedforward layer,
then add normalize.

96
00:05:49,390 --> 00:05:50,980
And in this case,
we're replacing

97
00:05:50,980 --> 00:05:54,430
the normal feedforward
layer with the switch layer.

98
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:57,140
And we can see an illustration
of this on the right.

99
00:05:57,140 --> 00:06:01,180
So on the right, we can see
that the layer has two inputs.

100
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:02,530
One is the token more.

101
00:06:02,530 --> 00:06:04,370
The other is the
token parameters.

102
00:06:04,370 --> 00:06:07,570
And we can see that these
embedding representations will

103
00:06:07,570 --> 00:06:09,910
get sent to a router, which
is exactly how it works

104
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:11,210
in the mixture of experts.

105
00:06:11,210 --> 00:06:12,752
So the router is
basically just going

106
00:06:12,752 --> 00:06:15,470
to be getting a distribution
over all of the experts.

107
00:06:15,470 --> 00:06:18,100
So in this case, we can see
that the highest probability

108
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:22,870
is going to expert number
two out of the four experts.

109
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:24,970
And then the right
token is actually

110
00:06:24,970 --> 00:06:28,390
having the most probability on
the first feedforward weight,

111
00:06:28,390 --> 00:06:30,273
which is like the first expert.

112
00:06:30,273 --> 00:06:32,440
So yeah, we can see here
that what we're going to do

113
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:34,565
is in the switch transformer,
which is very simple.

114
00:06:34,565 --> 00:06:37,900
It's just send it to the
highest probability expert.

115
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,630
And so here, we can see where
the adaptive computation lies,

116
00:06:40,630 --> 00:06:43,330
where we'll have
four sets of weights.

117
00:06:43,330 --> 00:06:45,593
There's some shared
weights and computation

118
00:06:45,593 --> 00:06:46,510
across all the tokens.

119
00:06:46,510 --> 00:06:48,052
For example, the
self-attention layer

120
00:06:48,052 --> 00:06:50,530
is computed exactly the
same for the more token

121
00:06:50,530 --> 00:06:52,720
and for the parameters token.

122
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:54,580
But in the sparse
switch layer, we

123
00:06:54,580 --> 00:06:56,038
can see that,
actually, the inputs,

124
00:06:56,038 --> 00:06:58,413
while having the same amount
of floating-point operations

125
00:06:58,413 --> 00:07:00,805
applied to them, actually have
different weight matrices.

126
00:07:00,805 --> 00:07:03,480

127
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:06,018
Next slide.

128
00:07:06,018 --> 00:07:07,810
Yeah, so that's the
kind of high-level idea

129
00:07:07,810 --> 00:07:10,450
with switch transformer
is that instead

130
00:07:10,450 --> 00:07:12,820
of sending a token to multiple
different experts, which

131
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:14,770
can also increase the
communication costs,

132
00:07:14,770 --> 00:07:16,300
as I'll go into a
little bit later,

133
00:07:16,300 --> 00:07:18,520
it also just significantly
kind of simplifies

134
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:22,520
the algorithm by just only
sending it to one expert.

135
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:24,880
So for the improved
training methodology,

136
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:26,770
we focused on three
different things

137
00:07:26,770 --> 00:07:29,570
to help improve the
training of sparse models.

138
00:07:29,570 --> 00:07:31,030
The first was
selected precision,

139
00:07:31,030 --> 00:07:33,070
which allows these
sparse models to be

140
00:07:33,070 --> 00:07:34,870
trained in lower-precision
formats, which

141
00:07:34,870 --> 00:07:36,380
is incredibly important.

142
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:38,658
Most of the models
we train, we really

143
00:07:38,658 --> 00:07:40,450
don't want to be using
float32 because it's

144
00:07:40,450 --> 00:07:41,920
just slower to compute.

145
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:43,990
And also, when you're
communicating tensors

146
00:07:43,990 --> 00:07:46,600
across different
processors and stuff,

147
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:49,493
it's twice as slow just because
there's twice as many things.

148
00:07:49,493 --> 00:07:51,160
Also, we have some
initialization tricks

149
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:52,690
and some training
tricks, as well,

150
00:07:52,690 --> 00:07:55,300
for allowing them to
be trained more stably,

151
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:56,960
especially as the
models grow in size,

152
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:58,930
which is like a new
initialization method,

153
00:07:58,930 --> 00:08:02,560
along with like a change to
the learning rate schedule.

154
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:05,320
And third, since our models
have so many more parameters,

155
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:08,600
we do notice definitely
different overfitting dynamics,

156
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:11,377
especially once we fine
tune these models that

157
00:08:11,377 --> 00:08:13,210
have been pretrained
on all of the internet,

158
00:08:13,210 --> 00:08:15,460
on these small tasks,
with maybe only 50,000

159
00:08:15,460 --> 00:08:17,680
to 100,000 examples, that
they can be much more

160
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:18,980
prone to overfitting.

161
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:23,080
So we also look at some
custom regularization

162
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:25,990
to help prevent some of the
overfitting that we observe.

163
00:08:25,990 --> 00:08:28,900
And finally, we also talk
about this differentiable load

164
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:31,660
balancing technique
we make, which kind of

165
00:08:31,660 --> 00:08:35,789
allows each expert to roughly
get the same amount of tokens.

166
00:08:35,789 --> 00:08:37,539
Because this is very
important, especially

167
00:08:37,539 --> 00:08:40,000
given that we want the stuff
to be efficient on hardware.

168
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,179
We want, roughly, each expert to
have similar amounts of tokens

169
00:08:43,179 --> 00:08:43,909
sent to it.

170
00:08:43,909 --> 00:08:45,850
And so to kind of
encourage this,

171
00:08:45,850 --> 00:08:48,820
we tack on an additional
load-balancing loss,

172
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:52,520
along with our cross-entropy
loss that we're training with.

173
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:53,980
Next slide.

174
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:56,470
OK, so here, I'm going to
go into selective precision.

175
00:08:56,470 --> 00:08:58,990
So, yeah, again, so when
we're training large models,

176
00:08:58,990 --> 00:09:00,910
it's really important that we
should be able to train them

177
00:09:00,910 --> 00:09:02,035
in lower-precision formats.

178
00:09:02,035 --> 00:09:05,920
So instead of each weight
and activation being 32 bits,

179
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:07,570
we want to shrink
it down to 16 bits.

180
00:09:07,570 --> 00:09:11,055
And we use the bfloat16
representation.

181
00:09:11,055 --> 00:09:12,430
And what we found
out of the gate

182
00:09:12,430 --> 00:09:15,570
is that these models
are just unstable,

183
00:09:15,570 --> 00:09:17,320
especially the sparse
models are much more

184
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:19,540
unstable than the dense
models in terms of you'll

185
00:09:19,540 --> 00:09:21,280
train it for 10,000,
20,000 steps,

186
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:23,030
and then the losses
would just diverge.

187
00:09:23,030 --> 00:09:25,400
This was something that
we frequently encountered.

188
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:27,550
And so one key
thing that we found

189
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:30,190
is that, basically,
you need to be casting

190
00:09:30,190 --> 00:09:35,110
a part of the computation
in float32 for these models

191
00:09:35,110 --> 00:09:37,750
to be able to be trained stably.

192
00:09:37,750 --> 00:09:40,600
And the key component that we
found that you need to cast

193
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:42,580
is the router computation.

194
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:45,983
And essentially-- we can go
into the technical details

195
00:09:45,983 --> 00:09:47,650
a little bit more
later-- but basically,

196
00:09:47,650 --> 00:09:50,230
any time that there's these
exponentiation functions,

197
00:09:50,230 --> 00:09:52,930
it's very important
that we are having

198
00:09:52,930 --> 00:09:56,080
higher and higher precision
because of round-off errors

199
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:59,230
that can then drastically
change the output of some kind

200
00:09:59,230 --> 00:10:01,400
of exponentiation function.

201
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:04,420
So for example, if you have
an exponentiation function

202
00:10:04,420 --> 00:10:07,090
and you change it by
0.1 or 0.2 or 0.3,

203
00:10:07,090 --> 00:10:11,290
this can drastically change the
output of exponentiating it,

204
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:14,142
especially depending on
how large the input is.

205
00:10:14,142 --> 00:10:15,850
So yeah, this was a
very important thing.

206
00:10:15,850 --> 00:10:17,933
And it basically doesn't
change the compute at all

207
00:10:17,933 --> 00:10:21,100
and allows the models to just
be significantly more stable.

208
00:10:21,100 --> 00:10:24,020
Next slide.

209
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:25,780
So the second thing
we looked at is also

210
00:10:25,780 --> 00:10:27,040
the initialization scale.

211
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:30,662
So the standard way that we
were initializing these models,

212
00:10:30,662 --> 00:10:32,620
we found to also just
make the models much more

213
00:10:32,620 --> 00:10:36,065
prone to being unstable
and/or just performing worse.

214
00:10:36,065 --> 00:10:38,440
So one thing that we did that
we found was very effective

215
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:41,140
was to just simply
make the initialization

216
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:42,350
scale much smaller.

217
00:10:42,350 --> 00:10:45,225
And when we did this, we
found that the quality just

218
00:10:45,225 --> 00:10:46,100
drastically improved.

219
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:48,760
And it was a very simple fix.

220
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:51,620
Next slide.

221
00:10:51,620 --> 00:10:53,400
And the third thing
I mentioned, where,

222
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:55,400
since we noticed that
these models are much more

223
00:10:55,400 --> 00:10:57,733
prone to overfitting, since
they just have significantly

224
00:10:57,733 --> 00:11:01,760
more parameters, is that we
also use much more dropout

225
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:03,120
for the expert layers only.

226
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:06,650
So here, we can see we
have the T5 base, which

227
00:11:06,650 --> 00:11:07,640
is a dense model.

228
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:09,560
And then we have a bunch of
different switch variants

229
00:11:09,560 --> 00:11:09,950
on that.

230
00:11:09,950 --> 00:11:12,200
And we've found to be the
most effective on these four

231
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:14,000
different fine
tuning tasks was just

232
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,920
to really significantly
increase the dropout

233
00:11:15,920 --> 00:11:17,630
rate inside the expert layers.

234
00:11:17,630 --> 00:11:20,180
And we found that this was
pretty effective for combating

235
00:11:20,180 --> 00:11:22,508
the overfitting.

236
00:11:22,508 --> 00:11:23,990
Barret?

237
00:11:23,990 --> 00:11:24,590
Yeah?

238
00:11:24,590 --> 00:11:26,480
We have a question from
one of the students.

239
00:11:26,480 --> 00:11:26,980
Oh, awesome.

240
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:27,500
OK.

241
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:29,580
OK, let me take a look.

242
00:11:29,580 --> 00:11:31,130
Do you want to go ahead?

243
00:11:31,130 --> 00:11:31,900
I can ask.

244
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:34,390
It was just, in reference
to that previous table where

245
00:11:34,390 --> 00:11:37,733
you have throughput
and precision,

246
00:11:37,733 --> 00:11:39,400
it just seemed
surprising to me that you

247
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:42,310
could match this 1390
number while using

248
00:11:42,310 --> 00:11:43,270
selective precision.

249
00:11:43,270 --> 00:11:46,880
It seems like I would expect
it to be something in between.

250
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:47,380
Yeah.

251
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:49,090
So essentially, it
comes down to the fact

252
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:51,970
that there's maybe a
little bit of noise

253
00:11:51,970 --> 00:11:53,110
sampled with the speed.

254
00:11:53,110 --> 00:11:57,220
And the only part we're casting
is the router, which is maybe

255
00:11:57,220 --> 00:11:59,950
such a insignificant
portion of the computation,

256
00:11:59,950 --> 00:12:01,522
and there's zero
communication there,

257
00:12:01,522 --> 00:12:03,980
that it's essentially like a
free operation in the network.

258
00:12:03,980 --> 00:12:06,610
So whether you cast it
to bfloat16 or float32,

259
00:12:06,610 --> 00:12:08,740
it doesn't actually
impact the speed at all,

260
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:12,280
within the precision that we
can actually measure the speed.

261
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:16,540
And also, these architectures
only use fast layer around one

262
00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:19,180
every four layers.

263
00:12:19,180 --> 00:12:23,140
And so, essentially,
the float32 policy

264
00:12:23,140 --> 00:12:27,217
is kind of very negligible
in the entire architecture.

265
00:12:27,217 --> 00:12:29,800
Yeah, it's like, for example, I
think, off the top of my head,

266
00:12:29,800 --> 00:12:33,130
it's like 1/40 the
computation that it would cost

267
00:12:33,130 --> 00:12:37,660
for you to do the first weight
matrix multiply in a ReLU

268
00:12:37,660 --> 00:12:38,877
dense layer or something.

269
00:12:38,877 --> 00:12:40,210
So it's a very, very small part.

270
00:12:40,210 --> 00:12:42,168
And yeah, we're not using
them very frequently,

271
00:12:42,168 --> 00:12:44,130
like Irwan mentioned, as well.

272
00:12:44,130 --> 00:12:44,630
Got it.

273
00:12:44,630 --> 00:12:45,190
OK, thanks.

274
00:12:45,190 --> 00:12:49,050

275
00:12:49,050 --> 00:12:49,550
Yeah.

276
00:12:49,550 --> 00:12:51,133
And then, just a
quick point on this--

277
00:12:51,133 --> 00:12:53,508
I won't go into some of the
technical details-- but yeah,

278
00:12:53,508 --> 00:12:55,580
we definitely, since we're
training these things

279
00:12:55,580 --> 00:12:56,750
on hardware and we really--

280
00:12:56,750 --> 00:12:58,958
I think a big part of the
mixture of experts paradigm

281
00:12:58,958 --> 00:13:01,370
is that these things are
designed such that it maps

282
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:03,590
really efficiently to hardware.

283
00:13:03,590 --> 00:13:05,930
So we want to be doing
dense matrix multiplies.

284
00:13:05,930 --> 00:13:07,970
And for this to
work really well,

285
00:13:07,970 --> 00:13:11,300
we also want to be able to have
roughly equal amount of tokens

286
00:13:11,300 --> 00:13:13,250
going to each of the
different experts.

287
00:13:13,250 --> 00:13:17,450
And I think this isn't that
sensitive to the load-balancing

288
00:13:17,450 --> 00:13:17,990
formulation.

289
00:13:17,990 --> 00:13:18,810
We tried a few things.

290
00:13:18,810 --> 00:13:19,685
A lot of them worked.

291
00:13:19,685 --> 00:13:21,200
But essentially,
you definitely want

292
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:26,030
some kind of load-balancing loss
added on when using sparsity.

293
00:13:26,030 --> 00:13:28,830
Yeah, next slide.

294
00:13:28,830 --> 00:13:30,520
Oh, that's me.

295
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:31,780
Yeah, Irwan, go ahead.

296
00:13:31,780 --> 00:13:39,443

297
00:13:39,443 --> 00:13:41,110
Yeah, so the frameworks
with the library

298
00:13:41,110 --> 00:13:45,040
we use rely on
static shapes for--

299
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:53,010

300
00:13:53,010 --> 00:13:59,730
so XLA, so the compiler for
TensorFlow and Mesh TensorFlow

301
00:13:59,730 --> 00:14:03,150
expect static
shapes for tensors.

302
00:14:03,150 --> 00:14:06,420
However, the computations
in switch transformers

303
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:11,610
are dynamic because
of the router, right?

304
00:14:11,610 --> 00:14:15,600
Different inputs will be
routed to different experts.

305
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:20,580
And so we need to specify ahead
of time how many tokens will

306
00:14:20,580 --> 00:14:22,470
be sent to each expert.

307
00:14:22,470 --> 00:14:27,720
And so we will introduce this
expert capacity hyperparameter

308
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:28,950
to specify that.

309
00:14:28,950 --> 00:14:32,130
And it's going to be
a static number which

310
00:14:32,130 --> 00:14:34,755
says how many tokens
each expert can process.

311
00:14:34,755 --> 00:14:37,990

312
00:14:37,990 --> 00:14:40,410
And so in practice, we
instead parameterize this

313
00:14:40,410 --> 00:14:44,620
by having a quantity
called the capacity factor.

314
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:45,780
So we have an example here.

315
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:48,750

316
00:14:48,750 --> 00:14:56,530
So the bottom row is a bunch
of tokens on one device.

317
00:14:56,530 --> 00:14:58,090
And then you need
to sort of route

318
00:14:58,090 --> 00:15:02,810
those tokens to multiple
devices, or multiple experts.

319
00:15:02,810 --> 00:15:06,520
So if too many tokens are
routed to a single expert,

320
00:15:06,520 --> 00:15:09,850
some tokens will be dropped
because, as we said,

321
00:15:09,850 --> 00:15:13,880
an expert has a fixed capacity.

322
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:15,490
So that's the
example on the left,

323
00:15:15,490 --> 00:15:17,200
where the capacity factor is 1.

324
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:21,730
And that basically means
that the toll does not

325
00:15:21,730 --> 00:15:27,070
like extra buffer
for routing tokens.

326
00:15:27,070 --> 00:15:29,440
So instead of that, we
can use a capacity factor

327
00:15:29,440 --> 00:15:30,550
that's larger than 1.

328
00:15:30,550 --> 00:15:34,610
So on the right, you
have an example with 1.5.

329
00:15:34,610 --> 00:15:38,650
So that means that now, each
expert has sort of three slots.

330
00:15:38,650 --> 00:15:42,500
They can process three tokens.

331
00:15:42,500 --> 00:15:44,500
And so that prevents
token dropping

332
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:46,790
because we have more capacity.

333
00:15:46,790 --> 00:15:50,860
But the issue is that
this means higher--

334
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:53,845
this means more expensive
communication across devices.

335
00:15:53,845 --> 00:15:58,030

336
00:15:58,030 --> 00:15:58,780
Yeah.

337
00:15:58,780 --> 00:16:01,840
OK, so-- yeah, go ahead.

338
00:16:01,840 --> 00:16:02,440
Yeah.

339
00:16:02,440 --> 00:16:04,450
So one thing that we
also experimented with

340
00:16:04,450 --> 00:16:07,750
was this method called
no token left behind.

341
00:16:07,750 --> 00:16:09,050
And the idea was the following.

342
00:16:09,050 --> 00:16:13,600
So since we have to have a
fixed batch size for each expert

343
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:15,580
and there can be token
dropping, we kind of

344
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:18,130
were thinking that, hey,
yeah, having tokens drop,

345
00:16:18,130 --> 00:16:20,470
or having some tokens not
having any computation

346
00:16:20,470 --> 00:16:23,600
applied to it is probably
hurting the model performance.

347
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:26,100
So what if we do a
multi-stage routing procedure?

348
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:27,600
So first, you do
the normal routing,

349
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:29,683
where it's like you send
each token to its highest

350
00:16:29,683 --> 00:16:30,580
probability expert.

351
00:16:30,580 --> 00:16:32,800
But then any dropped
tokens, you then

352
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:36,332
send to their second-highest
probability expert

353
00:16:36,332 --> 00:16:38,290
and so forth and so on,
where you can basically

354
00:16:38,290 --> 00:16:40,415
repeat this process to
guarantee that no tokens are

355
00:16:40,415 --> 00:16:41,800
being dropped.

356
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:43,720
Interestingly,
actually, this approach

357
00:16:43,720 --> 00:16:46,030
didn't empirically
improve model performance.

358
00:16:46,030 --> 00:16:47,950
If anything, it actually
kind of hurt it.

359
00:16:47,950 --> 00:16:51,110
And we thought that was
actually very interesting.

360
00:16:51,110 --> 00:16:53,560
And I think the intuition is
that once the model learns

361
00:16:53,560 --> 00:16:55,738
it wants to send a
token to one expert,

362
00:16:55,738 --> 00:16:58,030
it really wants to have that
computation applied to it.

363
00:16:58,030 --> 00:17:00,310
And just applying
some other computation

364
00:17:00,310 --> 00:17:03,250
doesn't have, at all,
the same property,

365
00:17:03,250 --> 00:17:06,349
along with it, actually, maybe
being potentially detrimental.

366
00:17:06,349 --> 00:17:08,349
So yeah, we thought that
was pretty interesting,

367
00:17:08,349 --> 00:17:10,510
as we were very optimistic
this would potentially

368
00:17:10,510 --> 00:17:11,680
get improved performance.

369
00:17:11,680 --> 00:17:13,690
But it ended up not really
making a difference.

370
00:17:13,690 --> 00:17:16,418
And we found this
quite surprising.

371
00:17:16,418 --> 00:17:17,460
We have a question from--

372
00:17:17,460 --> 00:17:21,270

373
00:17:21,270 --> 00:17:23,400
I think it will
actually kind of address

374
00:17:23,400 --> 00:17:25,829
the majority of the last
point that you brought up.

375
00:17:25,829 --> 00:17:30,510
I think when I think about a
mixture of experts, usually,

376
00:17:30,510 --> 00:17:32,910
they specialize in
different things, right?

377
00:17:32,910 --> 00:17:38,040
So I think this just a lot--

378
00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:42,990
I was just wondering if you
send it to the second best

379
00:17:42,990 --> 00:17:47,610
or whatever, what if
all of your tokens

380
00:17:47,610 --> 00:17:51,210
would be particularly
good for one expert,

381
00:17:51,210 --> 00:17:56,910
and then you only process,
let's say, 20% of your tokens.

382
00:17:56,910 --> 00:18:00,330
So that ends up being
better than rerouting them

383
00:18:00,330 --> 00:18:02,040
to anything else.

384
00:18:02,040 --> 00:18:03,090
Exactly.

385
00:18:03,090 --> 00:18:05,482
Yeah, so even if you're
dropping a lot of tokens,

386
00:18:05,482 --> 00:18:07,690
it's not beneficial to be
sending them to the second,

387
00:18:07,690 --> 00:18:09,145
third, or fourth best thing.

388
00:18:09,145 --> 00:18:10,770
And one, actually,
interesting property

389
00:18:10,770 --> 00:18:12,810
that we noticed about
these models is they're

390
00:18:12,810 --> 00:18:15,180
surprisingly robust
to token dropping,

391
00:18:15,180 --> 00:18:16,540
especially during fine tuning.

392
00:18:16,540 --> 00:18:18,540
So yeah, in the standard
paradigm, what we'll do

393
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:19,748
is we'll pretrain this thing.

394
00:18:19,748 --> 00:18:21,400
We'll have some
load-balancing loss,

395
00:18:21,400 --> 00:18:24,420
which makes the tokens
pretty balanced, actually.

396
00:18:24,420 --> 00:18:25,920
But then, during
fine tuning, where

397
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:28,080
it's like we really want to
fine tune it on a specific task,

398
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:29,903
we actually studied
this exact question.

399
00:18:29,903 --> 00:18:31,320
And we were studying,
does it help

400
00:18:31,320 --> 00:18:34,268
to have a load-balancing loss
during fine tuning or not?

401
00:18:34,268 --> 00:18:36,060
And so if you have the
load-balancing loss,

402
00:18:36,060 --> 00:18:37,530
yeah, that kind
of is encouraging.

403
00:18:37,530 --> 00:18:39,180
For the specific
task, we want to try

404
00:18:39,180 --> 00:18:41,970
to have all the experts be
used versus turning it off,

405
00:18:41,970 --> 00:18:44,778
where there's definitely
some prior specialization.

406
00:18:44,778 --> 00:18:46,320
And it's actually
much better to just

407
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:47,800
turn the auxiliary loss off.

408
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:51,060
And even if it's 60% to 70% of
the tokens are being dropped,

409
00:18:51,060 --> 00:18:52,980
that actually
performs much better

410
00:18:52,980 --> 00:18:55,470
than having all
the tokens balance.

411
00:18:55,470 --> 00:18:57,300
But doesn't a
load-balancing loss

412
00:18:57,300 --> 00:18:59,490
encourage, basically,
all the experts

413
00:18:59,490 --> 00:19:03,300
to learn very similar weights
and then just randomly

414
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:04,795
assign your tokens?

415
00:19:04,795 --> 00:19:06,420
Because then it
doesn't matter to which

416
00:19:06,420 --> 00:19:08,635
expert stuff is being sent to.

417
00:19:08,635 --> 00:19:10,260
So when we use the
load-balancing loss,

418
00:19:10,260 --> 00:19:12,160
the routing mechanism
is definitely learned.

419
00:19:12,160 --> 00:19:13,740
So the model definitely
is encouraged

420
00:19:13,740 --> 00:19:16,710
to choose an expert
that it wants to send it

421
00:19:16,710 --> 00:19:17,710
to for good [INAUDIBLE].

422
00:19:17,710 --> 00:19:18,210
Right.

423
00:19:18,210 --> 00:19:20,640
But if all the experts
learn the same weights,

424
00:19:20,640 --> 00:19:24,390
then the router learns,
basically, oh, it

425
00:19:24,390 --> 00:19:26,650
doesn't matter
where I send it to.

426
00:19:26,650 --> 00:19:28,830
So if you encourage
load balancing,

427
00:19:28,830 --> 00:19:32,670
you encourage, technically,
that you want any loss

428
00:19:32,670 --> 00:19:35,250
to fit with any expert, right?

429
00:19:35,250 --> 00:19:36,840
That's maybe the
extreme behavior

430
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:39,630
if you have a very high
sort of load-balancing loss

431
00:19:39,630 --> 00:19:41,010
coefficient.

432
00:19:41,010 --> 00:19:43,230
But in practice, that
coefficient is kind of tuned,

433
00:19:43,230 --> 00:19:47,670
and we observe that,
for small enough values,

434
00:19:47,670 --> 00:19:52,330
the router still learns
semantic, meaningful routing.

435
00:19:52,330 --> 00:19:52,830
Yeah.

436
00:19:52,830 --> 00:19:55,770
Because it's like a balance
between this cross-entropy loss

437
00:19:55,770 --> 00:19:57,182
and this load-balancing loss.

438
00:19:57,182 --> 00:19:58,890
And so, on one hand,
yeah, you definitely

439
00:19:58,890 --> 00:20:00,815
want to encourage the
model to be balanced.

440
00:20:00,815 --> 00:20:02,190
Then, on the other
hand, you also

441
00:20:02,190 --> 00:20:04,260
want it to just get good
empirical performance.

442
00:20:04,260 --> 00:20:07,710
And yeah, the model is able
to definitely, on one hand,

443
00:20:07,710 --> 00:20:09,390
learn and specialize
the experts where

444
00:20:09,390 --> 00:20:11,880
they have different weights such
that it's like, definitely, it

445
00:20:11,880 --> 00:20:13,830
expects certain tokens to
be sent to certain experts

446
00:20:13,830 --> 00:20:15,900
but, on the other hand,
still be reasonably balanced

447
00:20:15,900 --> 00:20:17,317
so that the models
are efficiently

448
00:20:17,317 --> 00:20:20,340
run on modern hardware.

449
00:20:20,340 --> 00:20:21,930
Exactly.

450
00:20:21,930 --> 00:20:24,240
And we also have a question
from the classroom.

451
00:20:24,240 --> 00:20:27,480
So the question I want to ask
is it seems to me like this

452
00:20:27,480 --> 00:20:28,890
is a very experimental talk.

453
00:20:28,890 --> 00:20:30,360
We're talking about
floating-point precision.

454
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:31,590
We're talking about
different approaches

455
00:20:31,590 --> 00:20:32,897
that currently work well.

456
00:20:32,897 --> 00:20:34,980
And whenever we're dealing
with empirical science,

457
00:20:34,980 --> 00:20:37,760
