00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:26,280
And I think the way that we
use these things maps really

508
00:22:26,280 --> 00:22:29,188
well to our modern hardware,
which is also very promising.

509
00:22:29,188 --> 00:22:31,230
And I think the way we
were kind of looking at it

510
00:22:31,230 --> 00:22:33,540
is sparsity is a first
step towards doing

511
00:22:33,540 --> 00:22:37,373
more interesting and general
adaptive computation.

512
00:22:37,373 --> 00:22:39,540
Because I think it's like
this stuff is complicated.

513
00:22:39,540 --> 00:22:42,330
And typically, starting from
something that works well

514
00:22:42,330 --> 00:22:46,170
is better than necessarily
trying something

515
00:22:46,170 --> 00:22:47,953
that's not necessarily
as proven out

516
00:22:47,953 --> 00:22:49,870
and then trying to get
it to work really well.

517
00:22:49,870 --> 00:22:52,120
So I think we're kind of
starting from sparsity, which

518
00:22:52,120 --> 00:22:54,060
Noam Shazeer and others
got to work really

519
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:55,862
well in the context of LSTMs.

520
00:22:55,862 --> 00:22:57,570
We were kind of
interested in, let's port

521
00:22:57,570 --> 00:22:58,830
some of this to transformers.

522
00:22:58,830 --> 00:23:00,130
Let's get it
working really well.

523
00:23:00,130 --> 00:23:01,350
And then, let's
slowly start expanding

524
00:23:01,350 --> 00:23:02,970
towards a lot of the
other natural questions

525
00:23:02,970 --> 00:23:04,800
that you mentioned,
where it's like, OK,

526
00:23:04,800 --> 00:23:07,180
whereas instead of
different weights per core,

527
00:23:07,180 --> 00:23:09,430
let's also maybe have a
different computation per core

528
00:23:09,430 --> 00:23:10,260
and all of this.

529
00:23:10,260 --> 00:23:11,760
So that's, I guess,
how we were kind

530
00:23:11,760 --> 00:23:14,310
of building the natural
build-up and progression

531
00:23:14,310 --> 00:23:15,760
of our research.

532
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:16,260
Got it.

533
00:23:16,260 --> 00:23:16,760
Cool.

534
00:23:16,760 --> 00:23:17,400
Thank you.

535
00:23:17,400 --> 00:23:17,900
Yeah.

536
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:20,108

537
00:23:20,108 --> 00:23:21,150
What do you think, Irwan?

538
00:23:21,150 --> 00:23:22,025
Anything else to add?

539
00:23:22,025 --> 00:23:25,480

540
00:23:25,480 --> 00:23:27,630
Yeah, I guess I kind of
see adaptive computation

541
00:23:27,630 --> 00:23:32,040
and sparsity as related
but separate things.

542
00:23:32,040 --> 00:23:34,740
So sparsity is more like
different parameters

543
00:23:34,740 --> 00:23:35,760
for each example.

544
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:37,530
And adaptive
computation might be

545
00:23:37,530 --> 00:23:40,320
more different amount of flux.

546
00:23:40,320 --> 00:23:42,450
And we have some of that
with the token dropping,

547
00:23:42,450 --> 00:23:48,900
but that's not the
main domain motivation.

548
00:23:48,900 --> 00:23:52,980
Definitely, as
Barret mentioned, I

549
00:23:52,980 --> 00:23:56,130
would say no one
really has figured out

550
00:23:56,130 --> 00:23:58,950
adaptive computation
yet for deep learning.

551
00:23:58,950 --> 00:24:03,363
And one reason is because
we have these accelerators

552
00:24:03,363 --> 00:24:04,970
where I expect, sort of--

553
00:24:04,970 --> 00:24:08,634

554
00:24:08,634 --> 00:24:13,440
we need to work with a batch,
like data parallelism, right?

555
00:24:13,440 --> 00:24:15,930
And all of our accelerators
and our frameworks

556
00:24:15,930 --> 00:24:18,960
use this SPMD
paradigm where we're

557
00:24:18,960 --> 00:24:25,008
kind of supposed to apply the
same computation to examples.

558
00:24:25,008 --> 00:24:26,550
And so if you look
at the literature,

559
00:24:26,550 --> 00:24:30,660
you have works like
universal transformers

560
00:24:30,660 --> 00:24:33,660
where they replaced the
feedforwards in the transformer

561
00:24:33,660 --> 00:24:36,150
by just a recurrent weight.

562
00:24:36,150 --> 00:24:39,540
And so it's kind of like
an LSTM on each token.

563
00:24:39,540 --> 00:24:42,540
And the LSTM can stop
at different times

564
00:24:42,540 --> 00:24:45,163
based on some criteria.

565
00:24:45,163 --> 00:24:46,830
But the way these
things are implemented

566
00:24:46,830 --> 00:24:49,590
is just through masking.

567
00:24:49,590 --> 00:24:51,900
Because it needs
to be implemented

568
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:55,380
in the SPMD programming style.

569
00:24:55,380 --> 00:24:57,720
And so definitely,
sparsity was kind of easier

570
00:24:57,720 --> 00:24:59,615
to get to work first.

571
00:24:59,615 --> 00:25:02,115
And also, there were some prior
results with LSTMs, so yeah.

572
00:25:02,115 --> 00:25:06,500

573
00:25:06,500 --> 00:25:08,712
In terms of the first
question, what's

574
00:25:08,712 --> 00:25:10,670
the research question
here, it's just like, oh,

575
00:25:10,670 --> 00:25:13,010
can we design more
efficient models?

576
00:25:13,010 --> 00:25:15,800
And sparsity is
this new axis that

577
00:25:15,800 --> 00:25:17,120
hasn't been explored that much.

578
00:25:17,120 --> 00:25:21,368
And, yeah, I think that
I'm happy with just setting

579
00:25:21,368 --> 00:25:22,535
the research question, yeah.

580
00:25:22,535 --> 00:25:26,270

581
00:25:26,270 --> 00:25:26,770
Great.

582
00:25:26,770 --> 00:25:28,300
OK.

583
00:25:28,300 --> 00:25:29,260
Yeah, so next slide.

584
00:25:29,260 --> 00:25:33,320

585
00:25:33,320 --> 00:25:35,320
Yeah, again, so kind of
putting it all together.

586
00:25:35,320 --> 00:25:38,830
So the switch transformer
layer selects an expert, just

587
00:25:38,830 --> 00:25:40,970
the top expert, and
then incorporates

588
00:25:40,970 --> 00:25:42,970
a bunch of the general
sparse model improvements

589
00:25:42,970 --> 00:25:46,150
to allow it to fine
tune better, allow

590
00:25:46,150 --> 00:25:49,260
it to be more
regularized, allow it

591
00:25:49,260 --> 00:25:51,010
to be trained with
lower-precision formats

592
00:25:51,010 --> 00:25:52,630
and a lot of technical
details to just get

593
00:25:52,630 --> 00:25:53,922
them training and working well.

594
00:25:53,922 --> 00:25:57,717

595
00:25:57,717 --> 00:25:59,550
So yeah, one thing that
we also wanted to do

596
00:25:59,550 --> 00:26:02,740
was a comparison between
top-1 and top-2 routing,

597
00:26:02,740 --> 00:26:07,202
since top-2 routing was
the most popular technique.

598
00:26:07,202 --> 00:26:09,660
And so here, we can see we have
two different dense models,

599
00:26:09,660 --> 00:26:10,785
trained on different sizes.

600
00:26:10,785 --> 00:26:13,860
And we're going to be looking
at the pretraining negative log

601
00:26:13,860 --> 00:26:14,490
perplexity.

602
00:26:14,490 --> 00:26:19,290
So yeah, the bigger
the number, the better.

603
00:26:19,290 --> 00:26:20,000
So next slide.

604
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:21,972

605
00:26:21,972 --> 00:26:23,430
And what we're
going to be doing is

606
00:26:23,430 --> 00:26:26,013
we're going to be studying them
at different capacity factors.

607
00:26:26,013 --> 00:26:28,020
So a capacity factor
of 2.0 basically

608
00:26:28,020 --> 00:26:30,150
means that there is enough
buffer for two tokens

609
00:26:30,150 --> 00:26:32,130
to be sent to every
single expert.

610
00:26:32,130 --> 00:26:34,590
And we're going to be comparing
top one versus top two

611
00:26:34,590 --> 00:26:37,050
routing and also
comparing their speeds,

612
00:26:37,050 --> 00:26:42,240
along with their time to
get some threshold quality.

613
00:26:42,240 --> 00:26:45,390
OK, so here, we can see in
the capacity factor 2.0 case

614
00:26:45,390 --> 00:26:49,410
that the MoE models outperform
switch transformer, which

615
00:26:49,410 --> 00:26:50,550
makes a lot of sense.

616
00:26:50,550 --> 00:26:54,390
Since switch transformer is
only sending a top-1 token

617
00:26:54,390 --> 00:26:58,490
to each expert, the mixture of
expert is sending two tokens.

618
00:26:58,490 --> 00:27:00,240
So that makes sense
that this extra buffer

619
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:02,610
will be disproportionately
beneficial for the mixture

620
00:27:02,610 --> 00:27:04,000
of expert models.

621
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:05,310
And so we noticed that.

622
00:27:05,310 --> 00:27:06,660
And next slide, or, yeah, next.

623
00:27:06,660 --> 00:27:09,820

624
00:27:09,820 --> 00:27:12,640
Now so the really interesting
parts for the top-1 routing

625
00:27:12,640 --> 00:27:14,930
becomes when we lower
the capacity factors.

626
00:27:14,930 --> 00:27:16,713
So having a high
capacity factor is

627
00:27:16,713 --> 00:27:18,130
bad for many
reasons, one of which

628
00:27:18,130 --> 00:27:21,850
is it really incurs more of
these communication costs

629
00:27:21,850 --> 00:27:24,100
for sending tokens to
the correct experts.

630
00:27:24,100 --> 00:27:25,810
It also incurs
more compute costs

631
00:27:25,810 --> 00:27:28,220
and also incurs a lot
of memory overhead.

632
00:27:28,220 --> 00:27:29,860
So if you can get
this lower, it's

633
00:27:29,860 --> 00:27:32,770
usually a very, very good thing.

634
00:27:32,770 --> 00:27:35,670
And so what we see here is that
switch transformer actually

635
00:27:35,670 --> 00:27:39,630
outperforms mixture of experts
when you have a lower capacity

636
00:27:39,630 --> 00:27:40,230
factor.

637
00:27:40,230 --> 00:27:45,030
And we can see that the
time to quality threshold,

638
00:27:45,030 --> 00:27:46,350
we get there much quicker.

639
00:27:46,350 --> 00:27:50,243
And so even across the 2.0
and the 1.25 capacity factors,

640
00:27:50,243 --> 00:27:52,410
the kind of Pareto optimal
thing we saw in our setup

641
00:27:52,410 --> 00:27:55,860
is to use switch transformer
at a lower capacity factor,

642
00:27:55,860 --> 00:27:58,470
just due to the fact that
while the quality is worse,

643
00:27:58,470 --> 00:28:00,060
a little bit worse,
on a step basis,

644
00:28:00,060 --> 00:28:01,440
it's just much faster to run.

645
00:28:01,440 --> 00:28:05,040
So it's kind of the
Pareto optimal decision.

646
00:28:05,040 --> 00:28:07,400
Next slide.

647
00:28:07,400 --> 00:28:10,220
And we could also be seeing
that, for capacity factor 1.0,

648
00:28:10,220 --> 00:28:12,440
again, we can see that this
really disproportionately

649
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:16,310
benefits switch transformer
and is even better on a Pareto

650
00:28:16,310 --> 00:28:19,820
standpoint than the
1.25 capacity factors.

651
00:28:19,820 --> 00:28:22,310
And interestingly,
since MoE also

652
00:28:22,310 --> 00:28:23,850
does a little bit
more computation,

653
00:28:23,850 --> 00:28:26,913
you can also just increase
the amount of compute

654
00:28:26,913 --> 00:28:28,080
done elsewhere in the model.

655
00:28:28,080 --> 00:28:30,538
And we can see that that's a
much more efficient allocation

656
00:28:30,538 --> 00:28:31,550
of compute.

657
00:28:31,550 --> 00:28:33,500
So yeah, overall,
our takeaway is

658
00:28:33,500 --> 00:28:37,190
that lower capacity
factors using top-1 routing

659
00:28:37,190 --> 00:28:41,870
is more Pareto-efficient than
using top-2 routing at higher

660
00:28:41,870 --> 00:28:43,680
capacity factors.

661
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:45,825
Next slide.

662
00:28:45,825 --> 00:28:48,100
Irwan, you can take it over.

663
00:28:48,100 --> 00:28:48,600
OK.

664
00:28:48,600 --> 00:28:53,760
So next, we'll look at how
switch transformer scales

665
00:28:53,760 --> 00:28:56,490
as a function of the number of
experts in the switch layers.

666
00:28:56,490 --> 00:28:59,100

667
00:28:59,100 --> 00:29:00,930
And so on the right
side here, you

668
00:29:00,930 --> 00:29:05,310
see a plot that shows
perplexity versus

669
00:29:05,310 --> 00:29:09,660
training steps for different
switch architectures,

670
00:29:09,660 --> 00:29:12,240
ranging from T5 base,
which is basically

671
00:29:12,240 --> 00:29:17,850
no expert or a single
expert, up to 128 experts.

672
00:29:17,850 --> 00:29:20,640
And so you see that, as we
increase the number of experts,

673
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:23,810
which also increases the
number of parameters,

674
00:29:23,810 --> 00:29:30,240
sparse parameters, you
get sort of speed-ups.

675
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:32,730
You get increasing speed-ups
above the dense baseline

676
00:29:32,730 --> 00:29:37,980
and also sort of diminishing
returns to multiplying,

677
00:29:37,980 --> 00:29:40,500
to increasing, the number
of experts, as well.

678
00:29:40,500 --> 00:29:44,490

679
00:29:44,490 --> 00:29:49,310
So the previous figure
was looking at perplexity

680
00:29:49,310 --> 00:29:50,630
versus training steps.

681
00:29:50,630 --> 00:29:54,680
Here, we look at perplexity
versus training time.

682
00:29:54,680 --> 00:30:00,200
So that includes all the
additional communication costs

683
00:30:00,200 --> 00:30:05,390
when you have more
experts, comparing

684
00:30:05,390 --> 00:30:07,490
to the dense baseline.

685
00:30:07,490 --> 00:30:10,940
And so this is for
switch-based or dense T5 base.

686
00:30:10,940 --> 00:30:17,390
And we observe up to 7X
speedups of our T5 base.

687
00:30:17,390 --> 00:30:21,260
And so just to,
maybe, contextualize

688
00:30:21,260 --> 00:30:25,340
these numbers, 7X
speedups in deep learning

689
00:30:25,340 --> 00:30:26,670
are pretty hard to obtain.

690
00:30:26,670 --> 00:30:33,470
And so I think this is
one of the results that

691
00:30:33,470 --> 00:30:36,830
can spark a lot of
interest in sparse models,

692
00:30:36,830 --> 00:30:39,020
even if it's only for
pretraining for now,

693
00:30:39,020 --> 00:30:44,090
just having that number is
maybe there's a significant--

694
00:30:44,090 --> 00:30:46,335

695
00:30:46,335 --> 00:30:48,710
there's something significant
that can be happening here.

696
00:30:48,710 --> 00:30:53,710

697
00:30:53,710 --> 00:30:55,450
OK, so sparse scaling laws.

698
00:30:55,450 --> 00:31:01,810
So here, we'll look at sort
of loss versus sparse model

699
00:31:01,810 --> 00:31:04,210
parameters, which are
increased by increasing

700
00:31:04,210 --> 00:31:06,820
the number of experts.

701
00:31:06,820 --> 00:31:11,560
And so similarly to the
neural scaling lab paper,

702
00:31:11,560 --> 00:31:18,370
we observed that as you
increase the parameters,

703
00:31:18,370 --> 00:31:22,930
the sparse parameters,
and keep the FLOPS fixed,

704
00:31:22,930 --> 00:31:26,410
you get consistent gains
but diminishing gains.

705
00:31:26,410 --> 00:31:31,200

706
00:31:31,200 --> 00:31:33,630
OK, so now, we're going to
compare expert parallelism

707
00:31:33,630 --> 00:31:34,590
and model parallelism.

708
00:31:34,590 --> 00:31:38,700
So we introduced sparsity,
or expert parallelism,

709
00:31:38,700 --> 00:31:42,340
as a new dimension
to scale models.

710
00:31:42,340 --> 00:31:44,460
But of course,
there's the other one

711
00:31:44,460 --> 00:31:48,540
for dense model, which is
simply model parallelism, where

712
00:31:48,540 --> 00:31:51,960
model weights are partitioned
across cores once they

713
00:31:51,960 --> 00:31:56,580
are above the maximum size that
you can feed on a single core.

714
00:31:56,580 --> 00:32:01,130

715
00:32:01,130 --> 00:32:06,030
All right, so to the left
is expert parallelism here.

716
00:32:06,030 --> 00:32:06,530
Yeah.

717
00:32:06,530 --> 00:32:09,230
So essentially, what
we're doing is yeah,

718
00:32:09,230 --> 00:32:11,360
we're kind of comparing
a switch-based model

719
00:32:11,360 --> 00:32:13,250
versus the dense-based.

720
00:32:13,250 --> 00:32:15,860
And we're also comparing
against the larger dense model

721
00:32:15,860 --> 00:32:18,620
that has used model parallelism.

722
00:32:18,620 --> 00:32:19,898
And we can see that--

723
00:32:19,898 --> 00:32:22,190
because basically, when we
want to scale up model size,

724
00:32:22,190 --> 00:32:24,620
we kind of have two axes that
we can either go through.

725
00:32:24,620 --> 00:32:26,370
We can either increase
the number of FLOPS

726
00:32:26,370 --> 00:32:28,302
by scaling through
model parallelism

727
00:32:28,302 --> 00:32:30,260
or increase the number
of parameters by scaling

728
00:32:30,260 --> 00:32:31,370
through sparsity.

729
00:32:31,370 --> 00:32:34,590
And so we can see that, even
compared to a dense model

730
00:32:34,590 --> 00:32:36,590
that's been scaled up
through model parallelism,

731
00:32:36,590 --> 00:32:38,480
that sparsity is
still, at this scale,

732
00:32:38,480 --> 00:32:42,740
a more effective way to scale up
the model by still getting 2.5X

733
00:32:42,740 --> 00:32:46,430
speedups over this larger dense
model that was using model

734
00:32:46,430 --> 00:32:48,060
parallelism.

735
00:32:48,060 --> 00:32:48,560
Cool.

736
00:32:48,560 --> 00:32:50,420
So next slide.

737
00:32:50,420 --> 00:32:51,230
Yeah.

738
00:32:51,230 --> 00:32:53,960
Basically, here, T5-large
is the dense model

739
00:32:53,960 --> 00:32:56,098
that uses model parallelism.

740
00:32:56,098 --> 00:33:00,010

741
00:33:00,010 --> 00:33:01,300
Barret, go ahead.

742
00:33:01,300 --> 00:33:02,410
OK.

743
00:33:02,410 --> 00:33:04,420
And so one thing that we
also wanted to look at

744
00:33:04,420 --> 00:33:06,580
is are these expert
models effective

745
00:33:06,580 --> 00:33:08,643
if you have a really
small amount of compute

746
00:33:08,643 --> 00:33:10,060
or just a small
amount of experts?

747
00:33:10,060 --> 00:33:12,130
So typically, when we're
designing these models,

748
00:33:12,130 --> 00:33:14,255
we have one expert per core.

749
00:33:14,255 --> 00:33:16,630
But if you don't have a large
cluster to run these things

750
00:33:16,630 --> 00:33:19,210
on-- let's say you just
have a GPU with two cores

751
00:33:19,210 --> 00:33:20,170
or something--

752
00:33:20,170 --> 00:33:22,270
I guess is having two
experts more effective

753
00:33:22,270 --> 00:33:23,750
than just a dense model?

754
00:33:23,750 --> 00:33:24,927
And the answer is yes.

755
00:33:24,927 --> 00:33:27,010
So we can see even pretty
good scaling properties,

756
00:33:27,010 --> 00:33:29,230
even with a tiny
amount of experts,

757
00:33:29,230 --> 00:33:31,480
which is very promising for
these models to be used,

758
00:33:31,480 --> 00:33:35,330
even in much lower
compute regimes.

759
00:33:35,330 --> 00:33:37,670
Next slide.

760
00:33:37,670 --> 00:33:38,870
Irwan, you want to go ahead?

761
00:33:38,870 --> 00:33:41,930
OK, yeah.

762
00:33:41,930 --> 00:33:47,450
So we'll look at
what things look

763
00:33:47,450 --> 00:33:51,940
like when we use different types
of parallelism, namely expert

764
00:33:51,940 --> 00:33:55,400
parallelism, to add experts,
model parallelism to shared

